plan SurfaceSimplification
title   Public surface simplification and compiled truth
status  proposed, under discussion
goal
    Shrink the public API to what an application author must write, and make every structural
    fact about an application statically extractable. Flow State combines statechart actors
    (XState style) with keyed network data (TanStack Query style) in one Runtime. Its organising
    principle is "truth is a compilation target": machines, their read graph, their network
    operations and Stories compile into one internal representation for tools and agents.

    The primary author is an AI coding agent. A human typically vibe-codes, cares about the UI in
    the browser and rarely reads code. The library's job is to make agent mistakes hard to write
    and easy to detect. Everything beyond the authored machine files exists for correctness at
    scale and for agents.

    This Plan is the working record of the simplification discussion. Settled answers appear under
    decisions and outcomes. Candidate changes not yet discussed appear under @O5 and the open
    questions. After the discussion settles, the outcomes are applied to the twelve capability
    Plans, ProjectDelivery and the Dispatch Desk reference. Deliberate core designs in those
    Plans stay unless a change is a clear simplification (@D10). Their binding runtime guarantees
    (atomic cuts, fencing, ordered turns, no replay, honest remote uncertainty) are kept.
outcomes
    O1  Structure is data; callbacks only compute values
        Settled by @D2 and @D7. Inert construction of an App yields its complete structure, without
        running user callbacks or parsing TypeScript source.

        Data declares structure: states, transition targets, guard identities, the operations a
        transition starts, outcome-to-event mappings, activities per state, timers, context reads,
        persistence opt-ins and concurrency policies. Callbacks only compute values: event
        payloads, memory patches, operation parameters, guard predicates, selectors and write data.
        A callback never chooses a target, an operation, a descriptor or an event type.

        This needs one adjustment to @plan.Statecharts.O3. Transition actions keep today's
        operation-family call form, but the actions field becomes a static list of declarations
        rather than a callback that returns a list. Only the parameters inside each declaration are
        computed. For example, actions: [O.saveTitle.commit(({ memory, event }) => params, {
        writes, outcomes: { success: E.SaveSucceeded, failure: E.SaveFailed } })]. An outcome
        references an event constructor, never a raw string, so it reads unambiguously as an event
        (@D18). The Runtime passes the result to that constructor, and a constructor argument
        mismatch is a type error. Writes follow the same rule, so the target family is
        static and only data and parameters are computed.
    O2  The machine file is the spec; renderers serve tools
        Settled by @D3 and @D6. The authored .ts machine file is the declarative description of an
        application's states and transitions. No separate spec file exists or is committed.

        Construction produces one internal representation of the application's structure. Renderers
        project it on demand, for example as JSON for agents and as Mermaid for diagrams. CLI checks
        and diffs read the same representation. It contains no user values, closures or secrets.
    O3  Composition is an acyclic upward read graph plus nested states
        Settled by @D4. A machine declares which machines it reads from, using its Definition's
        context selectors. Providers never declare their consumers. The reads form a directed
        acyclic graph: a consumer reads from machines above it, and a cycle is rejected before any
        actor is admitted, as @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6 already requires. Substates are nested
        states inside one machine; there are no spawned or parent-owned child actors, matching
        PackageContract DEL-002.

        Current specification, unchanged so far. App.M lists the closed set of machines; any actor
        of an unlisted machine is rejected as foreign. actorRef(machine, id) declares a stable actor
        identity outside React, so one shared actor exists per ref per Runtime, and several refs
        may name the same machine.

        Settled by @D11: each dependency is declared once. A stable ref declares its provider
        bindings as module data, for example actorRef(EditorMachine, "north/T-42", { context: {
        canEdit: SessionRef } }). ensureActor(ref, { input }) and useActorByRef(ref) take no
        wiring, and the conflicting-bindings failure case disappears. Local actors from
        createActor and useActor still pass bindings, because they have no ref. Settled by @D13:
        module is removed; app({ id, persistenceVersion, machines }) lists machines directly, and
        tooling groups by machine ID prefix.
    O4  Future playback stays possible
        Settled by @D5. Browser playback of Stories, where the UI operates itself and records
        transitions, is out of scope for this revision. Changes here must not preclude it. Stories
        stay executor-agnostic data, events stay serialisable plain values, and the internal
        representation stays extensible with new edge kinds such as UI triggers.
    O5  Authoring returns values and throws on invalid input
        Settled by @D8. definition and machine are self-contained functions that return their value
        directly, and throw a FlowError carrying canonical diagnostics when construction fails.
        Event constructors return the event envelope directly. Result wrappers leave the authoring
        surface, including the double Result/unwrapped exports per definition. This applies to
        @plan.Statecharts.O1 and @plan.Statecharts.O2. Payload validation on admission stays.
    O6  Settled surface removals
        - State paths flatten to S.OPEN.EDITING (@D12). Compound states are real tokens; token
        metadata sits under a symbol key, so child names cannot collide with token fields. The
        existing reserved-name checks stay. Applies to @plan.Statecharts.O2.
        - One FlowError replaces FlowUsageError, FlowPersistenceError, FlowDisposeError and
        FlowStoryExecutionError (@D14). It carries a closed code, canonical diagnostics, an
        optional cause, and Story evidence for Story execution failures. This resolves
        @plan.StoriesAndModels.Q1 and applies to @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O3.
        - useShallow is removed (@D15). useView already re-renders only when the selected value
        changes, using whole-value Object.is for scalars and fieldwise Object.is for plain records
        (@plan.ReactAndHosts.O3).
        - onMemory and onMemory.select are removed (@D16). Every continuing declaration lives in
        a state's activities, including the root state for whole-lifetime work. An activity is
        a static declaration with computed parameters and an optional when predicate, for
        example ticket: O.ticketById.subscribe(({ memory }) => params, { when: ({ memory }) =>
        memory.watching }). Identity, retention, replacement and release rules from
        @plan.Statecharts.O4 are unchanged; when false releases the slot. This also lets state
        activities read memory, which the reference already assumes and the rewrite source does
        not yet accept. The previous-value argument of onMemory.select is dropped.
        - One root can(snapshot, event) is the only eligibility form (@D17). Selector input is a
        snapshot, so the same import works inside useView; the bound can(event) in selectors is
        removed. invalidate and clear stay, adopting the @D7 static form.
        - Story process() stays the single explicit drain step (@D19).
        - Outcome members success, failure, defect and interrupt are each optional, and all four
        remain available to authors who want them (@D20). An omitted member sends no event. The
        operation's state still records the outcome, writes still apply on success, and an
        omitted failure or defect also publishes an actor issue. flow check reports a state that
        an unmapped outcome would leave with no exit.
        - Context reactions become a static list in the machine configuration (@D23):
        onContext: [{ select, when?, event }]. select is a pure value over context and memory;
        only context waves re-trigger it (@D24). when filters on (current, previous). event is a
        static constructor reference, called with (current, previous). The first selection stays
        silent, emitted events are ordinary mailbox turns, and a handler that chose between
        events becomes several reactions sharing a selection. The selection cannot read the
        consumer's own state; state conditions belong in the statechart. Worked examples are in
        explorations/context-reactions.ts.
        - Operation keys (@D25, @D26, @D27). The authored key projector stays; its parameter type
        is the key input that readers, writes and cancel accept, and execution parameters must be
        assignable to it. The projector returns a named record that may hold derived or
        normalised values. invalidate and clear take a family plus an optional partial key record
        and match every entry whose key contains those properties, replacing tags. Undefined
        properties are treated as absent, as in TanStack Query. An omitted projector gives the
        empty record. Keys are records only. A lint flags resource and stream parameter fields
        missing from the key input. Applies to @plan.Resources.O1, @plan.Resources.O4,
        @plan.Transactions.O1 and @plan.Streams.O1.
        - Transition guards return true or deny(code, context?) (@D28). deny takes a literal code
        as a const type parameter and an optional plain context object, for example
        deny("CreditLimitExceeded", { total, limit }). A guard never returns false, so every
        denial is explained. Possible codes per transition are read exactly from types;
        actual denials are recorded in evidence. can(snapshot, event) stays boolean, and
        can(snapshot, event, { explain: true }) reports the denied candidates with their codes
        and context, or that no handler exists. This adopts FUTURE_EXPANSIONS.md section 2.
        - Operation option details (@D30 to @D34):
        - Resources, transactions and streams all write mappers inside one outcomes container.
        Finite members are success, failure, defect and interrupt; continuing members are
        value, plus complete for streams, failure, defect and interrupt. This resolves the
        container half of @plan.Resources.Q1 and @plan.Streams.Q1.
        - Transaction concurrency is required; there is no omitted policy. This resolves
        @plan.Transactions.Q2.
        - Resource staleTime and gcTime are both required, with no library defaults. Their
        input type is Effect Duration.Input. This resolves the rest of @plan.Resources.Q1
        except tags and placeholders, and tags are superseded by partial-record invalidation.
        - A stream adapter returns an Effect Stream directly: subscribe(params, { signal }) =>
        Stream<V, E, R>. Effectful or scoped setup uses Stream.unwrap, whose Effect 4.0.5 type is
        unwrap(effect: Effect<Stream<A, E2, R2>, E, R>): Stream<A, E | E2, R2 | Exclude<R,
        Scope>>, so scoped acquisition needs no separate unwrapScoped (absent in v4). Push sources
        may use Stream.callback, Stream.fromAsyncIterable or Stream.fromEventListener. This
        resolves the adapter half of @plan.Streams.Q1.
        - Transaction and stream facts persist exactly when the owning actor persists. Their
        descriptors accept only persist: false, to exclude one operation from a durable actor.
        Resources keep their own persist: true opt-in for shared cache data.
        - Every time input across the library is Effect Duration.Input: Story advance, timer
        delay, staleTime and gcTime (@D35). advanceTo keeps absolute epoch milliseconds. This
        resolves @plan.StoriesAndModels.Q3.
        - Model traversal: stateKey receives the predicted snapshot with a state member, matching
        snapshots everywhere; explored counts distinct state keys visited (@D36). This resolves
        @plan.StoriesAndModels.Q2 and @plan.StoriesAndModels.Q4.
        - flow.config.ts at the project root replaces the behaviour gateway (@D37):
        defineConfig({ apps: [{ app: DeskApp, stories: "src/desk/**/*.story.ts" }, { app:
        AdminApp, stories: "src/admin/**/*.story.ts" }] }). Each entry pairs one App with a
        Story glob; the App's id namespaces its Stories. Every exported Story is discovered,
        and each Story declares its own stable id in its options. Duplicate ids within one App,
        duplicate App ids, and a Story whose App differs from its entry fail flow check. CLI
        commands select an App with --app when the config holds more than one. This replaces
        behavior({ app, stories }) and the --gateway option of @plan.CommandLine.O1.
        - Saved behaviour artifacts are removed (@D38). behavior build, render, diff and check
        become on-demand commands: a graph command renders from the live App, and a diff
        command builds two git revisions in memory and compares them. Nothing is written unless
        output is redirected. This applies @D6 to @plan.CommandLine.O1 and
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O4.
        - The inspect route exports four names (@D39): inspect(app) returning the internal
        representation, render(model, format), trace (import, export, summarise, diff) and
        attachInspector(runtime, sink). The other restoration names become CLI internals.
        - CLI leaves in this revision (@D40): story list, describe and run; trace summarize, proof
        and diff; graph; diff; check; explain. explain has a static mode listing candidate
        transitions and their possible deny codes for a machine, state and event, and an
        evidence mode that evaluates can(…, { explain: true }) on a Story checkpoint snapshot.
        coverage, paths and an MCP server come later.
        - Callback analysis records observed reads from Stories and inspection, labelled as
        observed (@D41). Source-inferred reads come later.
        - flow check reports construction diagnostics, duplicate or misplaced Story and App ids,
        context-graph cycles and persisted consumers of non-persisted providers as errors; and
        stuck states from unmapped outcomes, non-literal deny codes, parameter fields missing
        from a key input, unreachable states, unhandled events and send-then-checkpoint without
        process() as warnings (@D42).
        - Public operation and timer states drop generation; it stays in inspection and evidence
        (@D43). Documentation states guarantees in user terms rather than mechanism names.
        - Effect is used publicly where it owns the concept (@D44 to @D48). Event payloads are
        Effect Schema field records; input and memory are Schema field records; operation data
        and error schemas are required only where values cross a boundary (persisted resources
        and streams, SSR seeding); services and fixtures are Effect Layers, replacing
        Implementation and ImplementationError. Decisions are made on internal simplification
        and readability; no performance or agent-accuracy spike is required.
        - Built-in transition properties (a denied event and an unhandled event change nothing)
        and deny-code coverage are accepted, independent of the invariant authoring form (@D49).
        - Persistence becomes a plain options object { storage, scope, version, codec? } on
        runtimeSetup (@D21). The persistence() constructor, the app-level persistenceVersion and
        the filter are removed; per-declaration persist flags are the single selection source.
        - Public snapshots keep lifecycle and drop publication and store revisions, which move to
        inspection (@D22).
    O7  Candidate simplifications awaiting discussion
        These are proposals, not decisions. Items with a question ID are in the current round.

        Invariants: not yet adopted (@Q30).

        Dropped: named guards (deny codes label guards), parameter-only readers (superseded by
        @D25), createRuntime (same concept count as runtimeSetup plus construct), Story steps that
        settle by default (@D19), a machine redesign around Effect primitives (@D60), results
        handled in the waiting state (@D54), renaming updateMemory, and moving shared handlers to
        parent states.
decisions
    D1  Correct on first attempt outranks reviewability, which outranks brevity
        User answer, round 1. The API offers one canonical form per concept, even where a shorter
        alternative would be possible. Shorthands that create a second way to write the same thing
        are excluded; for example, transition targets stay required.
    D2  Structure-is-data is strict
        User answer, round 1. It is enforced by types and checks, with no dynamic escape hatch.
    D3  The machine file is the human-facing spec
        User answer, round 1. There is no separate spec file. Machine source should read
        declaratively. Further machinery serves correctness and agents, since humans mostly work on
        UI.
    D4  Hierarchy is upward reads plus nested states
        User answer, round 1. A child machine may read any machine above it. Parents do not
        declare children, and the read graph must stay acyclic. Nested states provide substates.
    D5  Playback is deferred
        User answer, round 1. It is not planned in this revision, but must remain buildable later.
    D6  One internal representation with renderers; nothing stored
        User answer, round 2. Structure is held in one internal representation and rendered on
        demand as JSON, Mermaid and similar formats. No generated spec artifact is committed or
        passed around.
    D7  Actions keep the operation-family call form as a static list
        User answer, round 2. Operations, writes and outcome-to-event mappings are declarations;
        only parameters and data are computed.
    D8  No Result returns from authoring
        User answer, round 2. Construction failure throws. Results were an earlier idea that may
        have served the Runtime internally; they are not exposed.
    D9  State targets stay typed object tokens
        User answer, round 2. Targets are typed nested state objects, not strings, to keep type
        safety. Only the path spelling is under discussion (@Q6).
    D10  Simplify, do not redesign
        User direction, round 2. Deliberate core designs stay. A change qualifies only when it
        removes concepts, names or ceremony for the author without weakening a settled decision.
    D11  Context wiring is declared once, on the stable ref
        User answer, round 3.
    D12  State paths flatten
        User answer, round 3. S.OPEN.EDITING replaces S.OPEN.S.EDITING; tokens stay typed objects.
    D13  module is removed
        User answer, round 3.
    D14  One FlowError
        User answer, round 3.
    D15  useShallow is removed
        User answer, round 3, accepted after confirming useView re-renders only on a changed
        selection. A component whose selection is unchanged does not re-render on a transition.
        A selector that builds a new array or nested object on each run still re-renders, with or
        without useShallow; memoised derivation or a lint rule addresses that case.
    D16  onMemory and onMemory.select are removed
        User answer, round 4. Root and state activities with computed parameters and a when
        predicate replace them.
    D17  One root can import
        User answer, round 4. can(snapshot, event) works everywhere, including selectors.
    D18  Outcomes reference event constructors
        User answer, round 4. Raw event-name strings are rejected because they read ambiguously as
        states or events.
    D19  process() stays
        User answer, round 4, consistent with the archived Story design: send only admits, and
        process() drains ready work, so Stories can queue commands and control interleavings.
    D20  Outcome mappers are optional; omission sends no event
        User answer, round 5.
    D21  Persistence is a plain object with version; filter removed
        User answer, round 5.
    D22  Snapshots keep lifecycle; revisions move to inspection
        User answer, round 5.
    D23  Context reactions are a static list with select, when and event
        User answer, round 6. Static analysability of select and when is reviewed under @Q16.
    D24  Reaction select may read memory
        User answer, round 6. Only context waves re-trigger selection.
    D25  Key input projector with named record keys and partial-record invalidation
        User answer, round 6, after comparison with TanStack Query.
    D26  Undefined key properties are absent
        User answer, round 6.
    D27  Keys are records only
        User answer, round 6.
    D28  Guards return true or deny(code, context?)
        User answer, round 8. The second argument is an optional context object.
    D29  guard and when stay separate
        User answer, round 8. guard is the event-transition condition; when is the context
        reaction filter. Redirect and timer keywords are unchanged.
    D30  One outcomes container across families
        User answer, round 9.
    D31  Transaction concurrency is required
        User answer, round 9.
    D32  staleTime and gcTime are required
        User answer, round 9.
    D33  Stream adapters return a Stream
        User answer, round 9, after checking Effect 4.0.5 source and declarations for
        Stream.unwrap and Stream.scoped.
    D34  Transaction and stream persistence follows the actor, with persist: false to exclude
        User answer, round 9.
    D35  Duration.Input is the only time input type
        User direction, round 10. No per-feature question is needed for time inputs.
    D36  Model stateKey uses state; explored counts distinct keys
        User answer, round 10.
    D37  flow.config.ts with multiple App entries and Story-declared ids
        User answer, round 10. A project may hold several Apps.
    D38  On-demand graph and diff replace saved behaviour artifacts
        User answer, round 10.
    D39  The inspect route has four exports
        User answer, round 10.
    D40  CLI core set plus explain
        User answer, round 11.
    D41  Observed callback reads now; inferred later
        User answer, round 11.
    D42  flow check rule set
        User answer, round 11.
    D43  generation leaves public state
        User answer, round 11.
    D44  Event payloads are Effect Schema field records
        User answer, round 12.
    D45  Input and memory are Schema field records
        User answer, round 12. Whether an initial function is needed is open.
    D46  Operation data and error schemas only where values cross a boundary
        User answer, round 12.
    D47  Layer replaces Implementation
        User answer, round 12.
    D48  No spike; decide on simplification and readability
        User direction, round 12.
    D49  Built-in transition properties and deny-code coverage
        User answer, round 12.
    D50  State tokens expose is
        User answer, round 15. S.OPEN.is(snapshot) is true for the state and any descendant; it
        resolves the open spelling of matches in @plan.Statecharts.O4.
    D51  Timer conditions use when
        User answer, round 15; superseded by @D53, which removes the timer condition entirely.
    D53  Timers become schedules with schedule and send
        User answer, round 18. timers is renamed schedules. Each named entry is { schedule, send }.
        schedule accepts Duration.Input (fires once), any Effect Schedule, or a function of the
        entry snapshot ({ memory, now }) returning either. send accepts an event envelope or a
        function of the schedule metadata ({ attempt, elapsed, … }) returning one event. delay,
        target, the timer guard or when, and any exhaustion event are removed: the receiving
        transition's guard filters, and results decide give-up. The Runtime steps schedules with
        Schedule.toStep on its own Clock, preserving timer snapshots, advanceToNextTimer,
        deterministic ordering, suspension and exit/re-entry rules. A plain duration restores
        exactly; a multi-step Schedule restarts on restore. The CLI may preview any Schedule by
        simulation, labelled simulated. Applies to @plan.Statecharts.O4.
    D52  Invariants stay a separate function
        User direction, round 16. Placing invariants on machine state nodes was rejected as noisy.
        The invariant syntax and runtime design are paused and will be revisited (@Q30).
    D54  Operation outcomes keep mapping to events
        User answer, round 19. Handling results directly in the waiting state is dropped; the
        runtime keeps one entry path for results, the outcome-to-event form of @D18 and @D30.
    D55  The definition derives public Schemas
        User answer, round 19. A definition exposes Event (a Schema.TaggedUnion), State (literals
        of state paths), Input, Memory and Snapshot, so application boundaries decode and encode
        with Flow's own protocol. States remain a tree of names. Applies to @plan.Statecharts.O1.
    D56  Events use the _tag envelope
        User answer, round 19. Events are the Schema.TaggedUnion itself, with its match and
        isAnyOf; the type field is removed.
    D57  Operation state lanes use _tag with PascalCase
        User answer, round 19; the match helper is settled by @D61. Applies to ResourceState, 
        TransactionState and StreamState in
        @plan.Resources.O1, @plan.Transactions.O1 and @plan.Streams.O1.
    D58  Failures map by error tag
        User answer, round 19. When an operation's error type is a tagged union, outcomes.failure
        is an exhaustive record from tag to event constructor; an untagged error type keeps the
        single-event form. The type decides which applies.
    D60  Keep the definition and machine shape
        User direction, rounds 14 to 15. Four redesign angles and a synthesised vision
        (explorations/machine-vision.ts) were reviewed and not adopted as a whole. The definition
        stays the cohesive vocabulary and the machine keeps its typed callback; changes land as
        small, case-by-case decisions (@D50 onwards). State-owned data is not adopted.
    D61  Operation states are matched with Effect's Match.valueTags
        User answer, round 20. Refines @D57: the _tag lanes need no Flow match helper and no new
        root export; consumers use Match.valueTags(state, cases) from Effect.
    D59  Memory requires an initial function
        User answer, round 19. initial converts input to memory, is required whenever memory is
        non-empty, runs once for a fresh actor and never on restore.
constraints
    C1  Keep binding runtime semantics
        Simplification removes names and ceremony, not guarantees. Ordering, atomic publication,
        fencing, no-replay restore, honest uncertainty and exact type inference from the capability
        Plans remain required, and their proofs remain owner obligations.
    C2  Proposal authority only
        This Plan changes no owner Plan, Task status, package export or reference file by itself.
        packages/flow-state stays frozen and unread for this work.
    C3  Code composition is the north star
        Public APIs and internal code must make the user's code-composition, to-spec,
        testing-policy and lint-bomb style principles easy to follow: concerns implemented by
        boundaries, protocols that carry established facts, complete contracts (input, returns,
        fails, needs), policy at the composition site, one fact in one place, requirements before
        invariants, and public-boundary tests. Effect usage follows the lint-bomb glossary.
        Installed Effect 4.0.5 is the API authority; codebases/effect-v4 is 4.0.0-beta.98 and can
        differ (for example Arbitrary.schema versus Schema.toArbitrary).
verification
    V1  Extractability review
        Establish @O1 by rewriting the Dispatch Desk reference in the agreed surface and deriving
        its internal representation by hand. Every transition target, operation, outcome edge and
        context read must be present without executing a callback; any fact that needs a callback
        is a failing case. Planned review; no representation builder exists yet.
    V2  Type feasibility spike
        Establish the type side of @O1, @O5 and any flattened path form by compiling them in an
        isolated consumer without explicit generics, assertions or excessive instantiation. Record
        compiler time per machine size. Planned; not run.
questions
    Q30  What does invariant-driven development mean here?
        Paused by the user. Invariants stay a separate function (@D52); syntax and runtime design
        will be revisited. Earlier findings: they would run at the pre-publication validation
        step in enforce, report or off mode.
